    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can you go deeper into the heat soak part? specifically how the thermal conductivity of the pad material influences the rate at which heat transfers to the caliper and what that does to the pedal feel during back-to-back high-speed braking.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The thermal conductivity of the pad material dictates how quickly the heat generated at the pad-rotor interface is transferred *into* the pad itself, and subsequently, how much of that heat is conducted into the caliper assembly. If the pad has lower thermal conductivity, it might retain more of the intense, localized heat, but the overall transfer rate to the caliper might be slower, which can sometimes be advantageous for initial pedal feel. Conversely, a material with higher conductivity acts like a better heat sponge, rapidly dumping that thermal energy into the caliper structure. This rapid heat transfer to the caliper is what directly impacts pedal feel, often manifesting as a softer, spongier, or less immediate pedal response due to thermal expansion and changes in hydraulic fluid properties within the caliper.",
      "source": "target",
