    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i've noticed in some testing that the brake systems occasionally act up or behave unpredictably even when the math seems right, like there's some random interference or sudden drop in pressure. what are the most common causes for those kinds of sudden, \"mischievous\" mechanical inconsistencies during a testing run?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden, unpredictable mechanical inconsistencies during testing, even when the theory is sound, are often caused by factors that are less about the core material science and more about the *system* itself. These \"mischievous\" behaviors are frequently related to fluid dynamics, mechanical binding, or thermal runaway in ancillary components. For instance, caliper piston sticking due to thermal expansion, or even minor air ingress into the hydraulic lines, can cause sudden pressure drops that feel like a complete system failure. Another possibility, especially with high-stress testing, is the subtle warping or uneven heating of the rotor itself, which changes the contact patch characteristics unpredictably.",
      "source": "target",
